Here is a detailed legal breakdown of the $10 billion federal civil rights lawsuit (Entertainment Studios Networks, Inc., et al. v. McDonald's Corporation, U.S. District Court for the Central District of California), expanding on the factual structure you outlined: [1] (https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/business/business-news/byron-allen-mcdonalds-settlement-discrimination-lawsuit-1236291018/), [2] (https://www.blackenterprise.com/byron-allens-allen-media-group-wins-significant-legal-victory-in-racial-discrimination-based-lawsuit-against-mcdonalds-corporation-in-u-s-federal-court/)

I. Parties & Procedural Posture

• Plaintiffs. Entertainment Studios Networks, Inc. (ESN) and Weather Group, LLC (parent company of The Weather Channel), both 100% African American-owned media companies controlled by Byron Allen. [1] (https://www.nbcnews.com/news/nbcblk/mcdonalds-ordered-face-byron-allens-10-billion-discrimination-lawsuit-rcna48753), [2] (https://millerbarondess.com/miller-barondess-represents-entertainment-studios-in-high-profile-civil-rights-litigation), [3] (https://peopleofcolorintech.com/articles/mcdonalds-settles-byron-allens-10-billion-racial-bias-lawsuit/), [4] (https://www.blackenterprise.com/byron-allens-allen-media-group-wins-significant-legal-victory-in-racial-discrimination-based-lawsuit-against-mcdonalds-corporation-in-u-s-federal-court/)
• Defendant. McDonald’s Corporation. [1] (https://www.blackenterprise.com/byron-allens-allen-media-group-wins-significant-legal-victory-in-racial-discrimination-based-lawsuit-against-mcdonalds-corporation-in-u-s-federal-court/)
• Court & Judge. U.S. District Court for the Central District of California, presided over by Judge Fernando M. Olguin. [1] (https://www.marketingdive.com/news/mcdonalds-plans-to-more-than-double-spending-on-diverse-owned-media-part/600530/), [2] (https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/business/business-news/byron-allen-mcdonalds-settlement-discrimination-lawsuit-1236291018/)
• Procedural History.
	• May 2021. Plaintiffs filed the initial $10 billion complaint.
	• November 2021. Judge Olguin granted McDonald's initial motion to dismiss without prejudice, finding the original complaint lacked sufficient factual detail to plausibly establish racial animus.
	• September 2022. After Plaintiffs filed an amended complaint with bolstered factual allegations, Judge Olguin denied McDonald's second motion to dismiss, ruling the case could move into discovery.
	• December 2024. Judge Olguin denied McDonald’s motion for summary judgment, clearing the case for a jury trial.
	• June 2025. Just weeks before the scheduled July 15, 2025 jury trial, the parties reached a confidential settlement. [1] (https://theblackwallsttimes.com/2024/12/05/byron-allen-10b-lawsuit-against-mcdonalds-approved-for-trial/), [2] (https://www.nexttv.com/news/judge-rejects-motion-to-dismiss-byron-allens-suit-vs-mcdonalds), [3] (https://finance.yahoo.com/news/mcdonalds-settles-byron-allens-10-184020900.html), [4] (https://www.nbcnews.com/news/nbcblk/mcdonalds-ordered-face-byron-allens-10-billion-discrimination-lawsuit-rcna48753), [5] (https://www.marketingdive.com/news/mcdonalds-plans-to-more-than-double-spending-on-diverse-owned-media-part/600530/), [6] (https://www.blackenterprise.com/byron-allens-allen-media-group-wins-significant-legal-victory-in-racial-discrimination-based-lawsuit-against-mcdonalds-corporation-in-u-s-federal-court/)

II. Core Factual Allegations

To survive dismissal, Allen’s amended complaint shifted focus from McDonald’s general public DEI statements to structural contracting barriers:
• The Segregated "African American Tier". Plaintiffs alleged McDonald’s split its $1.6 billion annual U.S. advertising budget into two tracks. The vast majority went to a "general market" tier managed by white-owned ad agencies, while Black-owned media were consigned to an "African American tier" managed by a separate agency with a tiny budget (allegedly less than $5 million total) and worse pricing. [1] (https://chicagocrusader.com/mcdonalds-settles-with-byron-allen-after-his-10b-lawsuit/), [2] (https://www.nrn.com/quick-service/mcdonald-s-settles-10-billion-racial-bias-lawsuit), [3] (https://www.nbcnews.com/news/nbcblk/mcdonalds-ordered-face-byron-allens-10-billion-discrimination-lawsuit-rcna48753)
• Racial Stereotyping of Broad-Appeal Networks. Despite operating mainstream, general-audience networks (such as The Weather Channel, Cars.TV, Comedy.TV, and Pets.TV), Plaintiffs alleged McDonald’s pigeonholed their networks as "Black media" solely because of Allen’s race, barring them from competing for general-market ad dollars. [1] (https://www.restaurantbusinessonline.com/marketing/mcdonalds-settles-discrimination-lawsuit-media-mogul-byron-allen-maintains-advertising), [2] (https://millerbarondess.com/miller-barondess-represents-entertainment-studios-in-high-profile-civil-rights-litigation), [3] (https://peopleofcolorintech.com/articles/mcdonalds-settles-byron-allens-10-billion-racial-bias-lawsuit/), [4] (https://finance.yahoo.com/news/mcdonalds-settles-byron-allens-10-184020900.html)
• Disparate Treatment. Plaintiffs alleged McDonald’s refused to contract with ESN since its 2009 founding—and cut ad spending on The Weather Channel after Allen acquired it in 2018—while routinely buying ads on smaller, white-owned networks with lower viewership ratings. [1] (https://www.franchisetimes.com/franchise_news/mcdonalds-settles-with-black-owned-media-group-ahead-of-set-trial-date/article_996eb5e9-1e3c-4266-b01f-06a66a9d0fa1.html), [2] (https://www.nexttv.com/news/judge-rejects-motion-to-dismiss-byron-allens-suit-vs-mcdonalds), [3] (https://millerbarondess.com/miller-barondess-represents-entertainment-studios-in-high-profile-civil-rights-litigation)

III. Legal Claims & Governing Standard

• Federal Claim (42 U.S.C. § 1981). Guarantees all persons the same right "to make and enforce contracts... as is enjoyed by white citizens." [1] (https://www.shb.com/intelligence/newsletters/nep/2020/20-04-section-1981-discrimination), [2] (https://www.blackenterprise.com/byron-allens-allen-media-group-wins-significant-legal-victory-in-racial-discrimination-based-lawsuit-against-mcdonalds-corporation-in-u-s-federal-court/)
• State Claim (California Unruh Civil Rights Act, Cal. Civ. Code § 51.5). Prohibits business establishments from discriminating against or refusing to buy from/contract with any person on the basis of race. [1] (https://www.blackenterprise.com/byron-allens-allen-media-group-wins-significant-legal-victory-in-racial-discrimination-based-lawsuit-against-mcdonalds-corporation-in-u-s-federal-court/)
• The "But-For" Causation Hurdle. Under the U.S. Supreme Court's 2020 ruling in Comcast Corp. v. National Association of African American-Owned Media (a case also brought by Allen), a Section 1981 plaintiff cannot merely show race was a "motivating factor." Plaintiffs must plausibly plead and prove that but for racial discrimination, the defendant would have contracted with them. [1] (https://www.gibsondunn.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/supreme-court-unanimously-holds-that-a-plaintiff-suing-for-discrimination-under-42-u-s-c-%C2%A7-1981-must-allege-but-for-causation.pdf), [2] (https://www.scotusblog.com/2020/03/opinion-analysis-court-sends-race-discrimination-case-back-to-lower-courts-requires-plaintiff-to-meet-higher-bar/)

IV. Court’s Reasoning (Why the Case Survived Dismissal & Summary Judgment)

• Plausible Inference of Intentional Discrimination. In denying McDonald’s motion to dismiss (2022) and summary judgment (2024), Judge Olguin found that Allen’s allegations—specifically the structural "tiering" system, the refusal to deal with The Weather Channel post-acquisition despite its broad demographics, and comparisons to white-owned competitors—created a triable issue of fact as to whether race was the "but-for" cause of McDonald's refusal to contract.
• Role of Public DEI Statements. While public DEI pledges alone did not create a contract, Plaintiffs used the contrast between McDonald's public diversity rhetoric and its internal "tiering" practices as circumstantial evidence of pretext—arguing McDonald's stated business reasons for rejecting Allen's networks were cover for racial stereotyping. [1] (https://www.nbcnews.com/news/nbcblk/mcdonalds-ordered-face-byron-allens-10-billion-discrimination-lawsuit-rcna48753), [2] (https://theblackwallsttimes.com/2024/12/05/byron-allen-10b-lawsuit-against-mcdonalds-approved-for-trial/), [3] (https://www.nexttv.com/news/judge-rejects-motion-to-dismiss-byron-allens-suit-vs-mcdonalds)

V. Resolution (June 2025 Settlement)

• Terms. Financial terms remained confidential, and McDonald’s admitted no wrongdoing.
• Commercial Outcome. McDonald’s agreed to continue purchasing advertising from Allen’s companies at market value in alignment with its commercial objectives. In a joint statement, Allen acknowledged McDonald’s commitment to investing in Black-owned media and dropped both the federal lawsuit and the appeal of his state fraud suit. [1] (https://finance.yahoo.com/news/mcdonalds-settles-byron-allens-10-184020900.html), [2] (https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/business/business-news/byron-allen-mcdonalds-settlement-discrimination-lawsuit-1236291018/), [3] (https://www.restaurantbusinessonline.com/marketing/mcdonalds-settles-discrimination-lawsuit-media-mogul-byron-allen-maintains-advertising), [4] (https://www.nrn.com/quick-service/mcdonald-s-settles-10-billion-racial-bias-lawsuit)
